From b8ae035d09a11e57f2017dc6853379472ba64092 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erni Sri Satya Vennela <ernis@linux.microsoft.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 21:45:22 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 3/9] Drivers: hv: vmbus: allow rescind messages to be handled
 concurrently

vmbus_connection.rescind_work_queue is created with create_workqueue(),
i.e. a per-CPU workqueue with max_active = 1, so at most one rescind is
processed per CPU at any time.  That serialization was never intentional,
and it deadlocks when several devices are hot-removed at the same time:

  CPU0                             CPU1
  ----                             ----
                                   netvsc_probe(NIC B)
                                     rtnl_lock()            <- acquired
                                     rndis_filter_device_add()
                                       vmbus_open(B)
                                         sends OPEN_CHANNEL
                                         wait_for_completion()
  [host rescinds NIC B: it now ignores the OPEN_CHANNEL for B]
  hv_vmbus_rescind worker
    vmbus_onoffer_rescind(NIC A)
      vmbus_rescind_cleanup(A)
      vmbus_device_unregister(A)
        netvsc_remove()
          rtnl_lock()             <- blocks, RTNL held by NIC B probe
  [rescind(B) queued behind rescind(A), never runs]

vmbus_rescind_cleanup(B) would set channel->rescind and wake the waiter in
__vmbus_open(), but it never runs, so both tasks are stuck and the guest
never sends CHANNELMSG_RELID_RELEASED.  Create the workqueue with
alloc_workqueue() and an unbounded concurrency level instead;
vmbus_onoffer_rescind() is already safe to run concurrently.  Do not carry
WQ_MEM_RECLAIM over: rescind processing is device teardown and is not
needed during memory reclaim, and it flushes workqueues of the drivers it
tears down, which do not set the flag.  create_workqueue() also sets
__WQ_LEGACY, which check_flush_dependency() exempts, so keeping
WQ_MEM_RECLAIM without it makes that pre-existing dependency warn:

  workqueue: WQ_MEM_RECLAIM hv_vmbus_rescind:vmbus_onmessage_work is
  flushing !WQ_MEM_RECLAIM hv_pci_cfca:0x0
  WARNING: kernel/workqueue.c:3921 at check_flush_dependency.part.0
   __flush_workqueue+0x39c/0x1160
   drain_workqueue+0x14b/0x350
   destroy_workqueue+0xb5/0x940
   hv_pci_remove+0x297/0x520
   vmbus_device_unregister+0x4f/0xf0
   vmbus_onoffer_rescind+0x5e9/0x700
   vmbus_onmessage_work+0x47/0x70

Signed-off-by: Erni Sri Satya Vennela <ernis@linux.microsoft.com>
---
 drivers/hv/connection.c | 9 ++++++++-
 1 file changed, 8 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/hv/connection.c b/drivers/hv/connection.c
index 818667ef9c74..ea6d74d34514 100644
--- a/drivers/hv/connection.c
+++ b/drivers/hv/connection.c
@@ -199,8 +199,15 @@ int vmbus_connect(void)
 		goto cleanup;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * Rescind messages for different devices must be able to make
+	 * progress independently of each other, so this workqueue is
+	 * created with an unbounded concurrency level rather than with
+	 * create_workqueue(), which would limit it to one work item per
+	 * CPU.  vmbus_onoffer_rescind() is safe to run concurrently.
+	 */
 	vmbus_connection.rescind_work_queue =
-		create_workqueue("hv_vmbus_rescind");
+		alloc_workqueue("hv_vmbus_rescind", WQ_UNBOUND, 0);
 	if (!vmbus_connection.rescind_work_queue) {
 		ret = -ENOMEM;
 		goto cleanup;
-- 
