From 9e20d3692c156934b4922cb4783ed4914de325c8 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Erni Sri Satya Vennela <ernis@linux.microsoft.com>
Date: Fri, 4 Sep 2026 21:45:26 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 9/9] net: hv_netvsc: do not wait forever for the host on a
 rescinded channel

hv_netvsc waits for the host in several places with a bare
wait_for_completion() or wait_event().  If the primary vmbus channel is
rescinded while such a wait is outstanding, the host stops processing the
ring buffer and the channel callback is torn down, so the event can never
arrive and the wait never returns.  Several of these waits run with RTNL
held, which turns the missed response into a deadlock with the rescind
handler, which needs RTNL to tear the device down:

  hv_netvsc ... eth0: sub channel open failed: -19

  task:kworker/1:1  state:D
  Workqueue: events netvsc_subchan_work [hv_netvsc]
    wait_for_completion+0x24/0x30
    rndis_set_rss_param_msg+0x1a3/0x200 [hv_netvsc]
    rndis_set_subchannel+0x295/0x320 [hv_netvsc]
    netvsc_subchan_work+0x45/0x120 [hv_netvsc]

  task:kworker/0:3  state:D
  Workqueue: hv_vmbus_rescind vmbus_onmessage_work
    rtnl_lock+0x15/0x20
    netvsc_remove+0x33/0x130 [hv_netvsc]
    vmbus_device_unregister+0x24/0x60
    vmbus_onoffer_rescind+0x236/0x250

The wait for outstanding sends to drain fails the same way, with the
rescind handler itself holding RTNL, so there is no cycle at all:

  task:kworker/0:1  state:D
  Workqueue: hv_vmbus_rescind vmbus_onmessage_work
    rndis_filter_device_remove+0x2d0/0x380 [hv_netvsc]
    netvsc_remove+0xfd/0x3a0 [hv_netvsc]
    vmbus_device_unregister+0x4f/0xf0
    vmbus_onoffer_rescind+0x59e/0x6b0

  addrconf_dad_work, netvsc_probe and ip(1044) all block on its RTNL.

Setting of chan->rescind by the vmbus driver does not wake these waiters,
so the condition has to be polled rather than added to the wait condition.
Add a netvsc_channel_rescinded() helper and a rndis_wait_for_response()
wrapper that polls the completion with a timeout and gives up with -ENODEV
once the primary channel is rescinded, and use it for the six RNDIS
request waits, for the NVSP_MSG5_TYPE_SUBCHANNEL completion and the
subchan_open wait in rndis_set_subchannel(), and for the wait_drain
waitqueue in rndis_filter_halt_device(), which is the wait that keeps the
guest from sending CHANNELMSG_RELID_RELEASED.  Finally, disable the
transmit path in netvsc_remove() before tearing the rndis device down, so
no new packets are queued to an already rescinded channel while the
outstanding completions are being drained.

Signed-off-by: Erni Sri Satya Vennela <ernis@linux.microsoft.com>
---
 drivers/net/hyperv/netvsc_drv.c   |  10 ++-
 drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c | 112 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 2 files changed, 108 insertions(+), 14 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/net/hyperv/netvsc_drv.c b/drivers/net/hyperv/netvsc_drv.c
index ab9f1cf6097a..b0a9dc690032 100644
--- a/drivers/net/hyperv/netvsc_drv.c
+++ b/drivers/net/hyperv/netvsc_drv.c
@@ -2678,8 +2678,16 @@ static void netvsc_remove(struct hv_device *dev)
 	if (vf_netdev)
 		netvsc_unregister_vf(vf_netdev);
 
-	if (nvdev)
+	if (nvdev) {
+		/*
+		 * Stop the tx path before tearing down the rndis device, so
+		 * no new packets are queued to a possibly already-rescinded
+		 * VMBus channel while rndis_filter_device_remove() is
+		 * draining outstanding completions.
+		 */
+		netvsc_tx_disable(nvdev, net);
 		rndis_filter_device_remove(dev, nvdev);
+	}
 
 	unregister_netdevice(net);
 	list_del(&ndev_ctx->list);
diff --git a/drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c b/drivers/net/hyperv/rndis_filter.c
index f8a4da2dea84..7528156fd04c 100644
